From: Runeon Contributors <dev@runeon.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mfmediaengine: Call the application's notify callback outside the engine lock.

The media engine called IMFMediaEngineNotify::EventNotify() with
engine->cs held, on the caller's thread in Play(), Pause(),
SetCurrentTime(), SetSource() and the setters, and on the work-queue
thread for session events. An application whose callback takes a lock
of its own, while another of its threads holds that lock and calls any
engine method, deadlocks: one thread holds the application's lock and
waits for engine->cs, the other holds engine->cs inside the callback
and waits for the application's lock. The deadlock was reported by
gauthierpiarrette/highball-engine (arm64 patch 0040), whose fix moves
every event to a work-queue thread; Wine's own tests show that Windows
delivers SEEKING and TIMEUPDATE during SetCurrentTime() on the calling
thread, so that design failed them. This is a different implementation.

Events are now queued with the thread that raised them, and each
thread delivers its own events, in order, as soon as it no longer
holds engine->cs: at the end of the call that raised them, or at once
for an event raised outside the lock. The callback therefore runs on
the same thread and before the call returns, as before and as on
Windows, but never under engine->cs. A thread that still holds the
lock recursively delivers nothing until it leaves it. Inside a
callback, only the events raised since it started are delivered, so
they come before the rest of the outer call's events, the order of
delivering each event as it was raised. Tearing a presentation down
does not deliver halfway; the caller's own unlock does. After
Shutdown() no event is queued, and queued events are dropped.

Limits: an event raised under engine->cs is delivered when the call
that raised it ends, so the callback sees the engine's state at the end
of that call, not between its steps (for example a ready state past
HAVE_METADATA in a LOADEDMETADATA callback, or LOADSTART after the
topology is built); events raised after Shutdown(), which were
delivered before, are dropped; a callback that leaves by an exception,
longjmp or ExitThread() leaves its barrier in the list, where the old
code left engine->cs held for good; events of different threads are no
longer serialized by
engine->cs, so two threads' callbacks can run at the same time and
interleave in another order than before; Shutdown() does not wait for
a callback already running on another thread, and an event that has
passed the shut-down check can still be delivered after Shutdown()
returns (the engine keeps its reference on the callback). Events still
queued when the engine is destroyed are freed undelivered.

